  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study's adjusted statistical models were unable to identify consistent, statistically significant housing predictors for indoor heat exposure or health symptoms, and the survey responses were not spatially or temporally paired with the microclimate observations.",
      "what": "This study monitored indoor and outdoor microclimates alongside household surveys in an informal settlement in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, during February and March. Outdoor daytime temperatures regularly exceeded 35°C, with 70% of monitored days reaching 'danger' levels on the Heat Index, while indoor Heat Index values frequently surpassed outdoor levels at night. Although over 75% of surveyed residents reported feeling hot and uncomfortable indoors, adjusted regression models did not find statistically significant relationships between specific housing characteristics—such as window size, roof color, ceilings, or vegetation—and indoor heat levels or self-reported health symptoms.",
      "why": "The study provides empirical, localized measurements of indoor-outdoor thermal dynamics in a sub-Saharan informal settlement, demonstrating that substandard housing fails to offer nighttime thermal relief and challenging the assumption that simple physical housing modifications easily translate to measurable indoor heat reduction in these dense settings."
    }
  },
